@anon.3c5d4856225436a016f648d8ca8332b3.2138 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2982 x i8] }> <{ [2982 x i8] c"# `auto_traits`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is [#13231]\0A\0A[#13231]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/13231\0A\0A----\0A\0AThe `auto_traits` feature gate allows you to define auto traits.\0A\0AAuto traits, like [`Send`] or [`Sync`] in the standard library, are marker traits\0Athat are automatically implemented for every type, unless the type, or a type it contains,\0Ahas explicitly opted out via a negative impl. (Negative impls are separately controlled\0Aby the `negative_impls` feature.)\0A\0A[`Send`]: ../../std/marker/trait.Send.html\0A[`Sync`]: ../../std/marker/trait.Sync.html\0A\0A```rust,ignore (partial-example)\0Aimpl !Trait for Type {}\0A```\0A\0AExample:\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(negative_impls)]\0A#![feature(auto_traits)]\0A\0Aauto trait Valid {}\0A\0Astruct True;\0Astruct False;\0A\0Aimpl !Valid for False {}\0A\0Astruct MaybeValid<T>(T);\0A\0Afn must_be_valid<T: Valid>(_t: T) { }\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    // works\0A    must_be_valid( MaybeValid(True) );\0A\0A    // compiler error - trait bound not satisfied\0A    // must_be_valid( MaybeValid(False) );\0A}\0A```\0A\0A## Automatic trait implementations\0A\0AWhen a type is declared as an `auto trait`, we will automatically\0Acreate impls for every struct/enum/union, unless an explicit impl is\0Aprovided. These automatic impls contain a where clause for each field\0Aof the form `T: AutoTrait`, where `T` is the type of the field and\0A`AutoTrait` is the auto trait in question. As an example, consider the\0Astruct `List` and the auto trait `Send`:\0A\0A```rust\0Astruct List<T> {\0A  data: T,\0A  next: Option<Box<List<T>>>,\0A}\0A```\0A\0APresuming that there is no explicit impl of `Send` for `List`, the\0Acompiler will supply an automatic impl of the form:\0A\0A```rust\0Astruct List<T> {\0A  data: T,\0A  next: Option<Box<List<T>>>,\0A}\0A\0Aunsafe impl<T> Send for List<T>\0Awhere\0A  T: Send, // from the field `data`\0A  Option<Box<List<T>>>: Send, // from the field `next`\0A{ }\0A```\0A\0AExplicit impls may be either positive or negative. They take the form:\0A\0A```rust,ignore (partial-example)\0Aimpl<...> AutoTrait for StructName<..> { }\0Aimpl<...> !AutoTrait for StructName<..> { }\0A```\0A\0A## Coinduction: Auto traits permit cyclic matching\0A\0AUnlike ordinary trait matching, auto traits are **coinductive**. This\0Ameans, in short, that cycles which occur in trait matching are\0Aconsidered ok. As an example, consider the recursive struct `List`\0Aintroduced in the previous section. In attempting to determine whether\0A`List: Send`, we would wind up in a cycle: to apply the impl, we must\0Ashow that `Option<Box<List>>: Send`, which will in turn require\0A`Box<List>: Send` and then finally `List: Send` again. Under ordinary\0Atrait matching, this cycle would be an error, but for an auto trait it\0Ais considered a successful match.\0A\0A## Items\0A\0AAuto traits cannot have any trait items, such as methods or associated types. This ensures that we can generate default implementations.\0A\0A## Supertraits\0A\0AAuto traits cannot have supertraits. This is for soundness reasons, as the interaction of coinduction with implied bounds is difficult to reconcile.\0A" }>, align 1
